      <h1>Zanzibar Malaria & Health Guide 2025</h1>
      <p class="blog-excerpt">Complete health guide for Zanzibar 2025. Malaria prophylaxis options, required vaccines, yellow fever rules, medical facilities, and travel insurance advice.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="blog-content">
      <section>
        <h2>⚠️ Disclaimer</h2>
        <p><strong>This guide is for informational purposes only. Consult a travel medicine specialist or your doctor 6–8 weeks before travel for personalized advice.</strong> Health recommendations change; verify with CDC, WHO, or your national travel health authority.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Malaria in Zanzibar</h2>
        <p>Zanzibar has <strong>year-round malaria transmission</strong> (mainly <em>Plasmodium falciparum</em>). Risk is present across the entire archipelago, including Stone Town and beach areas. Transmission peaks during/after rainy seasons (Mar–May, Nov–Dec).</p>
        
        <div class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;background:#fff3e0;border-left:4px solid #ff9800;margin:1.5rem 0;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">⚡ Key Point</h3>
          <p style="margin:0;"><strong>Malaria prophylaxis is strongly recommended for all travelers</strong> to Zanzibar, regardless of season or area visited.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Malaria Prophylaxis Options (2025)</h2>
        <table class="rate-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Medication</th><th>Dosing</th><th>Start Before</th><th>Continue After</th><th>Key Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Atovaquone-Proguanil (Malarone)</strong></td><td>1 tablet daily</td><td>1–2 days</td><td>7 days</td><td>Best tolerated; fewest side effects; take with food/fatty meal; expensive</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Doxycycline</strong></td><td>100mg daily</td><td>1–2 days</td><td>28 days</td><td>Inexpensive; sun sensitivity (use SPF50+); can cause yeast infections; take with large glass of water, stay upright 30min</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Mefloquine (Lariam)</strong></td><td>1 tablet weekly</td><td>2–3 weeks</td><td>4 weeks</td><td>Psychiatric side effects possible (anxiety, vivid dreams, depression); not for history of seizures/mental health issues</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        
        <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Atovaquone-Proguanil (Malarone) is generally first choice for short trips due to best side effect profile. Doxycycline is excellent budget option if sun sensitivity managed.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Required & Recommended Vaccines</h2>
        
        <h3>✅ Required</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Yellow Fever:</strong> <strong>ONLY if arriving from (or transiting >12h through) a yellow fever endemic country.</strong> Not required for direct flights from Europe, North America, Middle East, Asia. Certificate valid for life.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>💉 Strongly Recommended</h3>
        <table class="rate-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Vaccine</th><th>Schedule</th><th>Validity</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Hepatitis A</td><td>2 doses (0, 6–12 months)</td><td>20+ years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typhoid</td><td>Injection (1 dose) or oral (4 doses)</td><td>2–3 years / 5 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hepatitis B</td><td>3 doses (0, 1, 6 months)</td><td>Long-term</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rabies</td><td>3 doses (0, 7, 21–28 days)</td><td>Long-term (booster if exposed)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cholera</td><td>Oral (2 doses, 1–6 weeks apart)</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Meningococcal ACWY</td><td>1 dose</td><td>5 years</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        
        <h3>📋 Routine (Should Be Up to Date)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>MMR (Measles, Mumps, Rubella)</li>
          <li>Tdap (Tetanus, Diphtheria, Pertussis)</li>
          <li>Polio</li>
          <li>Influenza (seasonal)</li>
          <li>COVID-19 (current booster)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Other Health Risks</h2>
        
        <h3>🦟 Dengue & Chikungunya</h3>
        <p>Mosquito-borne, daytime biters (<em>Aedes</em>). No vaccine/prophylaxis. Prevent bites: DEET 30–50%, long sleeves, permethrin-treated clothing.</p>
        
        <h3>🚽 Traveler's Diarrhea</h3>
        <p>Common. Prevention: bottled/boiled water, peel fruits, cooked-hot food. Carry: loperamide (Imodium), oral rehydration salts, azithromycin (antibiotic for moderate/severe cases — get prescription).</p>
        
        <h3>☀️ Sun & Heat</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>SPF 50+ broad-spectrum, reef-safe sunscreen (reapply q2h)</li>
          <li>Wide-brim hat, UV sunglasses, UPF clothing</li>
          <li>Hydration: 3+ liters/day; electrolyte packets</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>🐠 Marine Hazards</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Stonefish, lionfish, sea urchins — wear reef shoes</li>
          <li>Coral cuts — clean immediately, antiseptic, monitor infection</li>
          <li>Jellyfish — vinegar for stings (not fresh water)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Medical Facilities in Zanzibar</h2>
        <table class="rate-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Facility</th><th>Location</th><th>Capabilities</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Mnazi Mmoja Hospital</td><td>Stone Town</td><td>Main public hospital; emergency, surgery, ICU; basic trauma</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Aga Khan Clinic</td><td>Stone Town</td><td>Private; good outpatient, lab, pharmacy; English-speaking</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zanzibar Medical Center</td><td>Stone Town</td><td>Private; 24h emergency, evacuation coordination</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Private clinics</td><td>Nungwi, Paje, Jambiani</td><td>Basic care, stabilization; refer to Stone Town for serious</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Serious cases:</strong> Medical evacuation to Nairobi (1.5h flight) or Dar es Salaam (20min flight) for advanced care. Ensure insurance covers medevac ($50,000–100,000+).</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Travel Insurance Checklist</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ Medical coverage: $100,000+ minimum</li>
          <li>✅ Medical evacuation: $250,000+ (to Nairobi/Dar/Johannesburg/India/Europe)</li>
          <li>✅ Repatriation of remains</li>
          <li>✅ round-the-clock emergency assistance line</li>
          <li>✅ Coverage for activities: diving, kite surfing, safari, horse riding</li>
          <li>✅ Pre-existing conditions declared</li>
          <li>✅ COVID-19 coverage (treatment, quarantine, cancellation)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Pack a Travel Medical Kit</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Antimalarials (full course + extra)</li>
          <li>Azithromycin 500mg (3–5 tablets) — prescription</li>
          <li>Loperamide (Imodium) + oral rehydration salts</li>
          <li>Antihistamine (cetirizine/loratadine)</li>
          <li>Hydrocortisone 1% cream (bites/rashes)</li>
          <li>Antiseptic wipes + antibiotic ointment (mupirocin/bacitracin)</li>
          <li>Waterproof plasters + blister pads</li>
          <li>DEET 30–50% or picaridin repellent</li>
          <li>Reef-safe SPF 50+ sunscreen</li>
          <li>Electrolyte packets (2–3/day)</li>
          <li>Personal prescriptions (in original bottles + copies)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

